n  as possible in order to understand user  behavior and sometimes that can include  future information that you don't have  at the time that that that the event  you're basing that metric on occurred  for example a lot of our users are  logged out when they access Airbnb and  we want to know if they're going to  convert to bookings so we want to go  back to the beginning and convert to say  that that log dot user was actually this  person who made this booking five days  later additionally our metrics are  needed entirely offline there's really  no online use case for a metric that  leads to different compute requirements  and finally there are a lot of types of  a lot of types of metrics who wanted to  support so in addition to the fact that  like standard metrics most of them you  just sum over like the number of times a  user clicked on something or at Airbnb  the number of times they booked a  listing I there are also the idea of  conversion metrics so how did how long  did it take for that new listing to get  its first booking we also frequently use  ratio metrics across the company I where  the numerator and denominator could be  from entirely different data sources on  the other hand ml features had their own  requirements so unlike where we wanted  to know as much information about our  metric as possible for ml you have a  very strict requirement to prevent data  leakage you do not want that future  information polluting your model and  because that can degrade the output and  if your model is in production you  experience in addition ml features need  to be available both online and offline  leads to a need for variable types of  compute and finally they have their own  complex types of calculations that they  need to support so between all of these  things we decided that they are  fundamentally different problems that  need fundamentally different  infrastructure to solve the basics are  similar but the details are different  and the things that are powerful about  these frameworks are really those  details it's we we actually are  beginning to come back around to revisit  this decision now that the global  metrics framework is built and up and  running we realize that this can  actually be an upstream input data  source for our ml models and we're  beginning to think about how that  integration might work so one of the  things that turned out to be pretty  different between our ml feature  requirements and our metric requirements  was the requirement for how they handled  dimensions so it Airbnb turns out  dimensions are really important and  there are a few reasons and I think that  they're going to be extensible to a lot  of other companies so first of all a lot  of the metrics we track across the  company are actually just dimensional  cuts of other metrics second a lot of  our metrics have hundreds of dimensions  that people might care to cut them by  and third we strongly believe in D  normalization for analytics purposes so  you might ask why do you believe so  strongly in de normalization for  analytics and it's because it makes it  so much faster also makes your data much  more reliable because if you have a  normalized data model like what is shown  at the top here every time you access  that data you need to remember how those  joins happen and you need to pay for the  cost of compute right then so it takes  much longer to execute that query and  get your  result back and frequently you just want  to know what God answer is but if you  have a to normalize model like what's  shown at the bottom the date is all  there it's easy to do your group eyes  and filters and it's especially much  easier for less technical folks like our  product managers who are frequently  accessing these datas to make this data  to make decisions  additionally like I said for this  framework we decided to leverage druid  and druid doesn't let you do joins so it  was a fundamental limitation we had to  normalize all of our data and the reason  that druid doesn't let you do joins is  because when the data is loaded in its  aggregated so here's some example dummy  data that you might see in droid I'm you  might notice there the timestamp column  some dimension columns and a metric  column but what's missing is an ID and  that's because you don't get that  performance if you load in atomic data  you would have to still pay that compute  cost at the time of querying it  so like I said another reason that we  had needed to handle this dimension  question was because a lot of our  metrics at Airbnb are actually just  dimensional cuts of other metrics for  example as a company we look at bookings  but the growth team looks at first-time  bookings our China team looks at  bookings in China and our Airbnb for  work team wants to know about how many  business trip bookings there were and  apologize so these are these are really  just dimensional cuts of bookings  additionally these are the metrics they  track but the power comes in when  they're able to do ad hoc exploratory  analysis cutting by any arbitrary  dimension so people don't just want to  know bookings in China today they might  want to know bookings in China from  North America by ho status tomorrow they  want to know might want to know bookings  in China by platform our arbitrary  dimensional cuts in order to understand  what's happening in our business so in  order to solve this problem we turn to a  familiar concept in data warehousing  that you are many of you are probably  familiar with we look to star schema and  in a star schema it's centered around  fact tables which are tables of atomic  events basically where your metrics live  and these join to dimension tables on  foreign keys to bring in that additional  context for our global metrics use case  we renamed these things a little bit our  fact tables became metric sources our  dimension tables became dimension  sources and we renamed the foreign key  subjects part of the reason 